<title>USA: Drier weather aids Minnesota fieldwork progress.</title>
<headline>Drier weather aids Minnesota fieldwork progress.</headline>
<dateline>ST PAUL, Minn. 1997-08-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>The following is the weekly crop summary from the Minnesota Agricultural Statistics Service based on conditions as of Sunday, August 3.</p>
<p>Most parts of the state welcomed a relatively dry, though humid week that reduced moisture problems on crops and allowed fieldwork to resume, according to the Minnesota Agricultural Statistics Service.</p>
<p>Scattered heavy rains kept fields too wet in a few areas. As of Friday, topsoil moisture was rated 1% very short, 4% short, 81% adequate, and 4% surplus. This year's combination of conditions has favored various weed, disease, and insect pests. Locally high populations of grasshoppers and army-worms are being reported in some areas. Second crop hay showed good yields where conditions were favorable for harvest, but a lot of hay is uncut or waiting in windrows for a sunnier, less humid day.</p>
<p>Small grain harvest is well underway across much of southern Minnesota, and about a week away from going full-scale in the southern part of the Red River Valley region. Scab is appearing in wheat fields in the Red River Valley due to recent wet, humid conditions. Areas which were dry through most of June are seeing low test weights. An estimated 76% of spring wheat is turning ripe versus 64% for the 5-year average. Only 2% of both spring wheat and barley, and 9% of oats, have been harvested to date.</p>
<p>As of Sunday, soybeans were 93% blooming, compared with 81% a year ago and 82% for the 5 year average for that date. Soybeans setting pods has reached 36%, equal to the 5-year average. Corn is 94% tasseling versus 75% for the 5 year average. Corn has reached milk stage on an estimated 16% of the acreage compared with 5% a year ago and 14% for the 5-year average.</p>
